---
// The home page (#415 (new site with the book)): the mockup that Pierre
// approved (project file design/moonpool-makeover/home-mockup.html), in its
// order of panels. It sells the tool: a hero, a live run of a seed, what you
// get, a test, your code and the providers, the techniques, used by paros.
//
// The run is the ping-pong demo (crates/moonpool-wasm-demo). The page shows
// the runs that `cargo test -p moonpool-wasm-demo --test site_runs` recorded
// in src/runs/home.json, then public/home.js loads the demo compiled to wasm
// and runs any seed in the browser.
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Panel from "../components/Panel.astro";
import { url } from "../nav";
import { builderCall, homeFigures, moonpoolDocTest } from "../lib/source.mjs";
import runs from "../runs/home.json";

const call = builderCall(moonpoolDocTest());
const test = `#[test]
fn server_accepts_under_chaos() -> Result<(), moonpool::SimulationError> {
${call.split("\n").map((l) => "    " + l).join("\n")}
    Ok(())
}`;
const figures = homeFigures();
const seeds = runs.map((r) => r.seed);
const TECHNIQUES = [
  ["One thread, one clock, one seed", "Run a seed twice and compare the two runs."],
  ["Same code", "One function, two sets of providers."],
  ["Network faults", "Cut a link, clog it, close a connection."],
  ["Storage faults", "What a crash does to each sector after the last sync."],
  ["Reboots", "A process starts again with fresh memory."],
  ["Swarm testing", "Each seed draws its own set of faults."],
  ["Assertions that must fire", "The coverage fills seed after seed."],
  ["Multiverse exploration", "The run forks at each discovery."],
  ["From a red seed to the bug", "Replay, then walk back to the first wrong event."],
];
---
<Base>
  <div class="home">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="kicker label"><span class="prompt">$</span> moonpool · deterministic simulation testing for Rust</div>
      <h1>One seed, one exact run<span class="cursor" aria-hidden="true"></span></h1>
      <p class="lede">Write your system once against provider traits. In tests, moonpool runs it in one thread on
        a simulated network, disk and clock, and injects faults. In production, the same code runs on Tokio.
        A failure gives you a seed, and the seed replays the failure.</p>
      <div class="actions">
        <button class="btn primary" type="button" id="hero-run">▶ Run a seed</button>
        <a class="btn" href={url("guide/quick-start/")}>Quick start</a>
        <a class="btn" href={url("guide/")}>The Sim Book</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="run" aria-label="A live run" id="run">
      <div class="run-head label">
        <span>seed <b id="r-seed">256</b> · run <span id="r-n">1</span> · <span id="r-src">recorded</span></span>
        <span>2 processes · Chaos::Network(ChaosMode::Random)</span>
      </div>
      <div class="run-stage"><svg id="stage" role="img" aria-label="The timeline of the run. The list of events under it holds the same facts."></svg></div>
      <div class="run-ctl">
        <span class="cap label">seed</span>
        {seeds.map((s) => <button class="btn small" type="button" data-seed={s}>{s}</button>)}
        <form id="seed-form" hidden>
          <label class="label" for="seed-input" hidden>Any seed</label>
          <input id="seed-input" inputmode="numeric" pattern="[0-9]*" placeholder="any seed" />
        </form>
        <span class="sep" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <button class="btn small" type="button" id="play">Play</button>
        <button class="btn small" type="button" id="step">Step</button>
        <button class="btn small" type="button" id="again">Run again</button>
      </div>
      <div class="run-stats" id="stats"></div>
      <div class="run-foot" id="foot"></div>
      <details class="log"><summary class="label">The events of this run</summary><pre id="log"></pre></details>
    </section>
    <p class="aside">The ping-pong demo of the repository (<a href={url("crates/#moonpool-wasm-demo")}><code>moonpool-wasm-demo</code></a>):
      a client sends 12 requests to a server over a simulated network with faults. The page first shows the
      recorded runs. Then it loads the simulator, compiled to wasm, and runs each seed in your browser.
      The network and the clock are simulated.</p>
    <script is:inline type="application/json" id="runs" set:html={JSON.stringify(runs).replace(/</g, "\\u003c")} />

    <div class="strip" aria-label="moonpool in figures">
      <div><span class="k">Provider traits</span><span class="v">{figures.providers.length}</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Chaos surfaces</span><span class="v">{figures.chaos.length}</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Assertion macros</span><span class="v">{figures.assertions.length}</span></div>
      <div><span class="k">Mocks</span><span class="v">none</span></div>
    </div>

    <Panel num="01" title="What you get">
      <ul class="caps">
        <li><b>A run you can replay</b><span>One seed feeds each random draw: the order of the tasks, the latency of each message, each fault. The same seed gives the same run.</span></li>
        <li><b>The same code</b><span><code>TokioProviders</code> in production, <code>SimProviders</code> in tests. No <code>Sim*</code> copy of your types, no test-only hook in the product.</span></li>
        <li><b>Faults by default</b><span>Partitions, clogs, torn writes, lost directory entries, reboots, slow machines, clocks that drift. Each seed draws its own set.</span></li>
        <li><b>Faults inside your code</b><span><code>hint!("batch durable, not sent")</code> names a moment. The simulator decides to strike there. <code>buggify!</code> forces a rare branch. Both do nothing in production.</span></li>
        <li><b>Assertions that must fire</b><span><code>assert_sometimes!</code> names a rare path. If no seed reaches it, the run fails: a fault that never fires tests nothing.</span></li>
        <li><b>Exploration</b><span>The explorer forks a run at each discovery and searches the timelines near it.</span></li>
        <li><b>Runs in a browser</b><span>The simulator has no syscall on its path, so it compiles to wasm. The run above is one.</span></li>
      </ul>
    </Panel>

    <Panel num="02" title="A test" meta="from the crate-level doc test">
      <div class="term">
        <div class="term-head label"><span>rust</span><span>ten seeds, network faults on</span></div>
        <Code lang="rust" code={test} />
      </div>
      <div class="term">
        <div class="term-head label"><span>rust</span><span>a seed failed: replay it alone</span></div>
        <Code lang="rust" code={"SimulationBuilder::new()\n    .set_debug_seeds(vec![failing_seed])\n    .set_iterations(1)"} />
      </div>
      <p class="aside"><a href={url("guide/quick-start/")}>Quick start</a>: the whole file, how to run it and how to read the report.</p>
    </Panel>

    <Panel num="03" title="Your code and the providers">
      <div class="arch" role="img" aria-label="Your code is generic over the provider traits. In production the traits are TokioProviders: real TCP, a real disk, a real clock. In tests they are SimProviders: a simulated network, disk and clock, all driven by one seed.">
        <div class="box">your code <small>generic over <code>P: Providers</code></small></div>
        <div class="arrow">▼ {figures.providers.map((p) => p.replace("Provider", "").toLowerCase()).join(" · ")}</div>
        <div class="two">
          <div class="box">TokioProviders <small>production: real TCP, disk and clock</small></div>
          <div class="box sim">SimProviders <small>tests: one thread, one seed, faults on</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Panel>

    <Panel num="04" title="See each technique work" meta="pages to come">
      <ol class="index-list">
        {TECHNIQUES.map(([n, d]) => <li><span class="n">{n}</span><span class="desc">{d}</span></li>)}
      </ol>
      <p class="aside">Until each page is up, the <a href={url("guide/")}>Guide</a> explains each technique in full.</p>
    </Panel>

    <Panel num="05" title="Used by paros">
      <p><a href="https://pierrez.github.io/paros/">paros</a>, a journal service on Paxos, runs whole cells of
        machines on moonpool: the shipped daemon, on the simulated disk and network, with each fault family on.
        Its CI runs a sweep of seeds on each pull request.</p>
    </Panel>
  </div>
  <script is:inline src={url("home.js")} data-wasm={url("wasm-demo/pkg/moonpool_wasm_demo.js")}></script>
</Base>
